UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 13, Lesson 2: Acting out a story', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"To act out a story, divide the parts: narrator, characters and a person to give cues. Dialogue is spoken by characters, and the narrator links the scenes. A cue is a signal for the next person to start, such as the last words of the line before. Rehearsing means repeating the scenes to remember the order, improve the voices and fix mistakes. Speak clearly and face the audience, and keep your body safe by agreeing on any action before you act. If you forget the line, say it in your own words and keep going.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nYour friend always comes in too early on stage. Write one way to help them find the cue and one way to recover if they forget a line.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Agree that their cue is the last word of my line, and say it slowly. If they forget their line, they can say it in their own words and keep the story going.'' A clear cue plus a recovery plan helps a team perform confidently.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving that actors must remember every word exactly. Knowing the story and staying in character matters more, and you can recover from mistakes by keeping the meaning.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite a one-page script of three scenes for your story from last lesson, with a narrator and two characters. Mark each cue. Rehearse it three times with a family member and write one thing that improved each time.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"To act out a story, divide the parts: narrator, characters and a person to give cues. Dialogue is spoken by characters, and the narrator links the scenes. A cue is a signal for the next person to start, such as the last words of the line before. Rehearsing means repeating the scenes to remember the order, improve the voices and fix mistakes. Speak clearly and face the audience, and keep your body safe by agreeing on any action before you act. If you forget the line, say it in your own words and keep going.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Create and perform","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-DRA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write a one-page script of three scenes for your story from last lesson, with a narrator and two characters. Mark each cue. Rehearse it three times with a family member and write one thing that improved each time. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using dialogue on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Believing that actors must remember every word exactly.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Agree that their cue is the last word of my line, and say it slowly.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing that actors must remember every word exactly. Knowing the story and staying in character matters more, and you can recover from mistakes by keeping the meaning. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating dialogue on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with dialogue, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about dialogue with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for dialogue. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for dialogue. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["''Agree that their cue is the last word of my line, and say it slowly.","Believing that actors must remember every word exactly.","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material.","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson."],"question":"Your friend always comes in too early on stage. Write one way to help them find the cue and one way to recover if they forget a line. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for dialogue; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of narrator and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Believing that actors must remember every word exactly.","To act out a story, divide the parts: narrator, characters and a person to give cues.","''Agree that their cue is the last word of my line, and say it slowly."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with dialogue?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named narrator and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing that actors must remember every word exactly.","Name narrator and explain why it matters only, without linking it to dialogue.","Apply dialogue to new material and name the narrator and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"Which response best applies dialogue with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on dialogue. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing that actors must remember every word exactly.","''Agree that their cue is the last word of my line, and say it slowly.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance."],"question":"Which response best uses elements, techniques and audience effect for dialogue?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Believing that actors must remember every word exactly.","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for dialogue.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about dialogue?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Example: Mia is the narrator: ''The two children searched under every log.'' Ben, as a child, says, ''It must be near the","Believing that actors must remember every word exactly.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — To act out a story, divide the parts: narrator, characters and a person to give cues. — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue."],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for dialogue?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name narrator and explain why it matters only, with no linked example.","Write a one-page script of three scenes for your story from last lesson, with a narrator and two characters.","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Believing that actors must remember every word exactly."],"question":"Which independent response best applies dialogue to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about dialogue with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating dialogue on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using dialogue on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for dialogue. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with dialogue, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Believing that actors must remember every word exactly.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Agree that their cue is the last word of my line, and say it slowly.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w13-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise dialogue.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Drama","success_criteria":["I can use dialogue with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify narrator and explain why it matters.","I can practise a cue independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a rehearsal needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"To act out a story, divide the parts: narrator, characters and a person to give cues.","options":["To act out a story, divide the parts: narrator, characters and a person to give cues.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about dialogue with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Mia is the narrator: ''The two children searched under every log.'' Ben, as a child, says, ''It must be near the stream!'' His cue is Mia''s last word, ''log''. They rehearse three times, checking that Ben starts on the cue and speaks clearly at the audience. In the second rehearsal Ben forgets and says, ''We should look by the water'', which works.\n\nTRY FIRST\nYour friend always comes in too early on stage. Write one way to help them find the cue and one way to recover if they forget a line.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Agree that their cue is the last word of my line, and say it slowly. If they forget their line, they can say it in their own words and keep the story going.'' A clear cue plus a recovery plan helps a team perform confidently.","explain":"To act out a story, divide the parts: narrator, characters and a person to give cues. Dialogue is spoken by characters, and the narrator links the scenes. A cue is a signal for the next person to start, such as the last words of the line before. Rehearsing means repeating the scenes to remember the order, improve the voices and fix mistakes. Speak clearly and face the audience, and keep your body safe by agreeing on any action before you act. If you forget the line, say it in your own words and keep going.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing that actors must remember every word exactly. Knowing the story and staying in character matters more, and you can recover from mistakes by keeping the meaning. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Narrator and dialogue.","caption":"Split the parts.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Narrator</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">links the scenes</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Dialogue</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">characters speak</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Mia is the narrator: ''The two children searched under every log.'' Ben, as a child, says, ''It must be near the s Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Mia is the narrator: ''The two children searched under every log.'' Ben, as a child, says, ''It must be near the s Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about dialogue with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Mia is the narrator: ''The two children searched under every log.'' Ben, as a child, says, ''It must be near the stream!'' His cue is Mia''s last word, ''log''. They rehearse three times, checking that Ben starts on the cue and speaks clearly at the audience. In the second rehearsal Ben forgets and says, ''We should look by the water'', which works. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"A cue and recovery.","caption":"Keep the story going.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Cue</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">last word of a line</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Recover</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">say it in your own words</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about dialogue with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving that actors must remember every word exactly. Knowing the story and staying in character matters more, and you can recover from mistakes by keeping the meaning. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Mia is the narrator: ''The two children searched under every log.'' Ben, as a child, says, ''It must be near the stream!'' His cue is Mia''s last word, ''log''. They rehearse three times, checking that Ben starts on the cue and speaks clearly at the audience. In the second rehearsal Ben forgets and says, ''We should look by the water'', which works.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Agree that their cue is the last word of my line, and say it slowly. If they forget their line, they can say it in their own words and keep the story going.'' A clear cue plus a recovery plan helps a team perform confidently.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Believing that actors must remember every word exactly. Knowing the story and staying in character matters more, and you can recover from mistakes by keeping the meaning. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","options":["Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about dialogue with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTo act out a story, divide the parts: narrator, characters and a person to give cues. Dialogue is spoken by characters, and the narrator links the scenes. A cue is a signal for the next person to start, such as the last words of the line before. Rehearsing means repeating the scenes to remember the order, improve the voices and fix mistakes. Speak clearly and face the audience, and keep your body safe by agreeing on any action before you act. If you forget the line, say it in your own words and keep going. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 13, Lesson 2: Acting out a story","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Role, character and storytelling","week":13,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Mia is the narrator: ''The two children searched under every log.'' Ben, as a child, says, ''It must be near the stream!'' His cue is Mia''s last word, ''log''. They rehearse three times, checking that Ben starts on the cue and speaks clearly at the audience. In the second rehearsal Ben forgets and says, ''We should look by the water'', which works.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Narrator and dialogue.","caption":"Split the parts.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Narrator</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">links the scenes</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Dialogue</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">characters speak</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A cue and recovery.","caption":"Keep the story going.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Cue</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">last word of a line</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Recover</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">say it in your own words</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w13-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
